usb - what is plugged in

  usb                    the devices on the bus, as a tree
  usb ports              every root port, whether or not anything came of it
  usb controllers        how many host controllers are driven

The bus is drawn the way `tree` draws a directory, because it is the
same kind of thing: a device on a hub is shown under that hub. Where it
sits reads as the controller and then every port down to it, so 1-1.3
is the third port of a hub on the first port of the second controller.

The last column is what the device calls itself, which is the only one
it wrote: a device that does not say leaves it blank rather than being
given a name here.

Class is what a device says it is. A device answering `per interface`
puts the truth in its interfaces instead, and the bus copies that up so
this column always says something useful.

The driver column is the device manager's answer, not the bus service's
opinion: the bus reads the descriptors, hands the class, vendor and
product to the manager, and prints whichever manifest matched. `none`
means the machine sees the device and has nothing to run against it,
which a manifest in /lib/drivers and `driver rescan` will fix.

What a driven device then becomes belongs to whatever it is. A disk
appears in `disk` as a volume and is mounted under /media, so a stick is
read like any other drive. A keyboard's keys arrive as keys, meaning
whatever the current layout says they mean, and a mouse moves the
pointer. None of this is the bus's doing; it only found them.

A hub is a device like any other and has a driver like any other; what
is behind it is enumerated the same way a root port's device is, and
appears and disappears the same way. A hub with nothing being plugged
into it costs no interrupts.

`usb ports` is the view for when a device is plugged in and does not
appear: a port that reads connected with no address against it is one
the machine sees and could not enumerate, which is a different fault
from a port with nothing in it. A port reading `companion` handed its
device to another controller, which is what happens to a full or low
speed device on a high speed bus. That controller is driven too, so the
device appears against it rather than being lost: the first column says
which controller found it.

Devices appear and disappear on their own. Nothing polls: a controller
interrupts when a port changes, the bus walks only that controller, and
a device sitting still costs nothing at all.
